          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Apart from medication, what helps with long-term insomnia?",
          "answer": "To manage long-term insomnia without medication, several lifestyle changes and non-pharmacological strategies can be helpful:\n\n### Sleep Habits\n1. **Consistent Wake Time**: The most important aspect is to wake up at the same time every day, even on weekends. This regularity helps to regulate your body clock.\n2. **Daytime Light Exposure**: Getting morning light can reinforce your internal clock, making it easier to sleep at night.\n3. **Limit Naps**: If you nap, do so early in the day and keep it short to avoid disrupting nighttime sleep.\n\n### Evening Routine\n1. **Wind Down**: Establish a calming pre-sleep routine. Engage in relaxing activities like reading or taking a warm bath.\n2. **Avoid Stimulants**: Reduce or eliminate caffeine and nicotine, especially in the afternoon and evening.\n\n### Environment\n1. **Sleep Environment**: Create a comfortable sleep environment. This includes a dark, quiet, and cool room.\n2. **Limit Screen Time**: Reduce exposure to screens (phones, TVs, computers) before bed, as blue light can interfere with your ability to fall asleep.\n\n### Physical Activity\nRegular physical activity can help reduce insomnia. Aim for at least 150 minutes of moderate exercise weekly, but avoid intense exercise close to bedtime as it might keep you awake (source: lifestyle-generalized-anxiety-disorder-activity).\n\nThese measures are not standalone treatments for chronic insomnia but can support better sleep when discussed alongside professional guidance (source: lifestyle-insomnia-sleep). \n\nFor further personalized advice, it is important to consult a healthcare professional.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
